    "content": "Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed has assembled a new cabinet as he begins a fresh five-year mandate, following recent national elections. The new Council of Ministers includes representatives from various segments of Ethiopian society, with the Somali region receiving one ministerial position.\n\nEngineer Sultan Wali has been appointed as Minister of Irrigation and River Basins, marking him as the sole Somali representative in the new federal cabinet. Wali previously served as State Minister of Water and Energy, bringing relevant experience to his new role.\n\nThe appointment comes at a time when Ethiopia is focusing on water resource management and agricultural development. The Ministry of Irrigation and River Basins plays a crucial role in overseeing major projects, including those in the Somali region where water scarcity and irrigation are key concerns.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant as it reflects the ongoing political representation of ethnic Somalis within Ethiopia's federal structure. The Somali region, also known as the Ogaden, has long sought greater inclusion in national decision-making processes. Wali's appointment may signal continued engagement with Somali interests in Addis Ababa.\n\nObservers note that the composition of the new cabinet will be closely watched for its impact on regional balance and the implementation of development policies. The Somali diaspora often looks to such appointments as indicators of how their communities' needs are prioritized in Ethiopia's governance.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/08/abiy-axmed-oo-dhisay-xukuumadda-cusub-iyo-soomaalida-heshay-hal-wasiir/",
