    "title": "Houthi Attacks on Saudi Airports Kill Three, Injure 36",
    "original_title": "Sucuudiga oo Sheegay in 3 qof ay ku dhinteen Weerarro lagu qaaday laba Garoon Diyaaradeed",
    "slug": "houthi-attacks-on-saudi-airports-kill-three-injure-36",
    "summary": "At least three people were killed and 36 injured in attacks on two Saudi airports claimed by Houthi rebels, according to Saudi aviation authorities.",
    "content": "Saudi Arabia's General Authority of Civil Aviation (GACA) reported that at least three people were killed and 36 others injured in attacks on two airports in the kingdom. The attacks targeted Abha International Airport and King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh on Tuesday and Wednesday, according to GACA. The Houthi movement claimed responsibility for the assaults.\n\nThe attack on Abha airport resulted in the deaths of two women, identified as nationals of Morocco and Algeria, and left 28 others wounded. No immediate details were provided about the casualties at King Khalid International Airport. The Houthis have previously launched similar attacks on Saudi infrastructure, including airports and oil facilities, as part of their ongoing conflict with the Saudi-led coalition in Yemen.\n\nThis escalation comes amid heightened tensions in the region, with the Houthis frequently targeting Saudi territory in what they describe as retaliation for the coalition's airstrikes in Yemen. The Saudi-led coalition has been fighting the Houthis since 2015, aiming to restore the internationally recognized Yemeni government. The conflict has caused a severe humanitarian crisis in Yemen, with thousands of civilians killed and millions displaced.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom live and work in Saudi Arabia, these attacks raise concerns about safety and security. Saudi Arabia hosts a significant Somali expatriate community, and any disruption to air travel or civilian infrastructure could directly affect their livelihoods and ability to travel. The targeting of airports, in particular, may lead to increased security measures and potential flight delays or cancellations, impacting diaspora members who rely on these hubs for travel to and from Somalia and other destinations.\n\nAdditionally, the involvement of the Houthis, who have ideological and alleged material ties to certain regional actors, adds a layer of complexity for the Somali diaspora, given the group's opposition to the Saudi-led coalition. Many Somalis in Saudi Arabia are economic migrants who send remittances back home, and any instability in the kingdom could have ripple effects on their families in Somalia. The international community has repeatedly called for de-escalation, but the cycle of violence persists, leaving civilians and expatriates vulnerable.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/08/sucuudiga-oo-sheegay-in-3-qof-ay-ku-dhinteen-weerarro-lagu-qaaday-laba-garoon-diyaaradeed/",
