    "id": "news-1791396526380-0",
    "title": "Eritrean Troops Reported in Ethiopia's Tigray as Tensions Escalate",
    "original_title": "Eritrean troops seen in Ethiopia’s Tigray as fears of wider conflict grow",
    "slug": "eritrean-troops-reported-in-ethiopia-s-tigray-as-tensions-escalate",
    "summary": "Witnesses say Eritrean soldiers were seen in the northern Ethiopian town of Adigrat, raising concerns of a wider conflict after diplomatic ties between Eritrea and Ethiopia were severed.",
    "content": "Two witnesses in the northern Ethiopian town of Adigrat reported seeing Eritrean soldiers driving through the area on Wednesday, according to Reuters. This would mark the first confirmed presence of Eritrean troops on Ethiopian soil since fighting erupted last month between Ethiopia's federal government and a rebel alliance in the Tigray region. The reported sightings come just days after Eritrea and Ethiopia cut diplomatic relations, heightening fears of a broader war.\n\nThe diplomatic rupture follows long-standing tensions between the two Horn of Africa neighbors. Eritrea gained independence from Ethiopia in 1993 after a decades-long war, and a bitter border conflict from 1998 to 2000 left deep scars. A 2018 peace deal, brokered under Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed, briefly eased relations and even earned Abiy the Nobel Peace Prize. However, recent months have seen a sharp deterioration, with both sides accusing each other of supporting opposing forces in the Tigray conflict.\n\nEritrea's Information Minister Yemane Gebremeskel denied that Eritrean forces were present in Ethiopia, calling the witness accounts false. The Ethiopian federal government has not commented on the reports. The rebel alliance, which includes the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF), has previously accused Eritrea of aiding Ethiopian federal forces—a claim Eritrea has consistently rejected.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the escalating crisis in Ethiopia carries significant implications. Ethiopia is a key player in regional stability and hosts a large Somali refugee population. Many Somali families have relatives in Ethiopia, and any wider conflict could disrupt trade routes and humanitarian corridors that are vital for Somalia. Additionally, Ethiopia's military is heavily involved in peacekeeping efforts in Somalia, and a full-scale war with Eritrea could divert attention and resources away from those operations.\n\nInternational observers are urging restraint. The African Union and the United Nations have called for dialogue to prevent further escalation. Yet with diplomatic channels closed and troops reportedly on the move, the situation remains volatile. The coming days will be critical in determining whether the region slides back into a devastating war.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/africa/eritrean-troops-seen-ethiopia-tigray-wider-conflict/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/africa/eritrean-troops-seen-ethiopia-tigray-wider-conflict/",
