    "slug": "constitutional-review-committee-reminds-government-of-october-14-deadline-for-implementing-articles-81-f-and-117-3a",
    "summary": "The Independent Committee for Constitutional Review and Implementation has reminded the Somali government that provisions separating members of parliament and ministers from holding dual roles will take effect on October 14, 2026.",
    "content": "Mogadishu, October 7, 2026 — The Independent Committee for Constitutional Review and Implementation (GMDHD) issued a press statement on Tuesday reminding the federal government that the six-month period allocated for aligning administrative measures with the new constitution expires on October 14, 2026. The committee, chaired by Avv. Burhaan Aadan Cumar, specified that starting from that date, Article 81, Paragraph 1, Subsection (f), and Article 117, Paragraph 3(a) of the Somali Federal Constitution will be enforced.\n\nThese articles address the separation of roles between members of parliament and ministers, prohibiting individuals from simultaneously holding both positions. The committee's statement did not provide further details on the specific content of the articles, but the reminder underscores the approaching deadline for compliance.\n\nThe constitution was adopted following a lengthy review process aimed at strengthening governance structures. The Independent Committee for Constitutional Review and Implementation has been tasked with overseeing the implementation of these provisions. The six-month transition period was intended to allow the government to adjust its administrative framework accordingly.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these constitutional changes are significant as they may affect the composition of the federal government and the separation of powers. Many diaspora members follow political developments closely and may have stakes in how governance is structured, particularly regarding accountability and the roles of elected officials versus appointed ministers. The implementation could influence how diaspora communities engage with or are represented in Somali political processes.\n\nThe committee's reminder serves as a final notice before the provisions take effect. It remains to be seen how the government will respond to ensure compliance by the October 14 deadline. The enforcement of these articles could mark a notable step in the ongoing implementation of Somalia's constitution.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/07/guddiga-madaxabannaan-ee-dib-u-eegista-iyo-hirgelinta-dastuurka-oo-xusuusisay-xukuumadda-in-14-ka-oktoobar-ay-dhaqan-gelayaan-qodobbada-kala-saaraya-xildhibaannada-iyo-wasiirrada/",
