    "id": "news-1791396544228-3",
    "title": "Eritrean Troops Reported in Ethiopia's Tigray Region, Raising Fears of Wider Conflict",
    "original_title": "Ciidamo Eriteriyaan ah oo lagu arkay Tigray, xiisadda Itoobiya oo isu beddelaysa khilaaf goboleed",
    "slug": "eritrean-troops-reported-in-ethiopia-s-tigray-region-raising-fears-of-wider-conflict",
    "summary": "Residents and local sources say Eritrean forces have entered towns in northern Ethiopia, heightening concerns that the post-war situation in Tigray could escalate into a broader regional conflict.",
    "content": "Residents in Ethiopia's northern Tigray region have reported seeing Eritrean troops in recent days, according to local sources. The soldiers were observed in the town of Adigrat, with some reportedly moving south toward Mekelle, the regional capital. The reports, relayed by the AFP news agency, have not been independently verified by Ramadpay Daily News.\n\nThe movements come as Ethiopian federal forces have redeployed in and around Mekelle, following the end of the two-year war between Ethiopia's government and Tigrayan forces in November 2022. That conflict, which killed hundreds of thousands, was formally concluded with a peace agreement signed in Pretoria. Eritrean troops fought alongside Ethiopian federal forces during the war but were not party to the ceasefire and have remained a contentious issue.\n\nEritrea's presence in Tigray has been a persistent source of tension since the cessation of hostilities. The 2022 agreement called for the withdrawal of foreign forces, but Asmara has never confirmed its troops were there. In the years since, reports of Eritrean incursions have periodically surfaced, often coinciding with political rifts between Ethiopia's federal government and Tigray's interim administration.\n\nThe latest sightings add to worries that unresolved disputes over territory and political autonomy could reignite fighting. Tensions between Ethiopia and Eritrea have also grown, with Ethiopian officials accusing Asmara of destabilizing the region. The African Union and international partners have urged restraint but have not publicly commented on these specific reports.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the developments carry significant weight. Many Somalis remember Eritrea's role in training and arming groups during Somalia's civil war, and any wider conflict in the Horn of Africa could disrupt trade routes and diaspora remittances. Additionally, Ethiopia's internal instability often spills over into Somalia, affecting security and humanitarian conditions. Diaspora communities in the United States, Europe, and the Middle East are closely watching, concerned that a new front could divert attention from Somalia's own challenges.\n\nAs of now, neither the Ethiopian federal government nor Eritrean authorities have issued statements about the reported troop movements. Ramadpay Daily News will continue to monitor the situation and provide updates as more information becomes available.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/07/ciidamo-eriteriyaan-ah-oo-lagu-arkay-tigray-xiisadda-itoobiya-oo-isu-beddelaysa-khilaaf-goboleed/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
    "source_name": "Goobjoog",
    "original_url": "https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/07/ciidamo-eriteriyaan-ah-oo-lagu-arkay-tigray-xiisadda-itoobiya-oo-isu-beddelaysa-khilaaf-goboleed/",
    "country": "Somalia",
