    "summary": "The Somali House of the People failed to convene on 7 October 2026 because not enough lawmakers attended, marking another postponement in recent weeks.",
    "content": "The House of the People of Somalia's Federal Parliament was unable to hold its scheduled session on 7 October 2026 after the required number of legislators did not show up. This is the latest in a series of cancellations caused by a lack of quorum.\n\nParliamentary rules stipulate that a session can only proceed if a minimum number of members are present. When that threshold is not met, the speaker must adjourn the sitting. The previous session on 28 September also failed to convene for the same reason, with only 123 lawmakers in attendance at that time.\n\nRepeated postponements have delayed legislative work, including debates and votes on key bills. The House of the People is one of the two chambers of Somalia's Federal Parliament, and its inability to meet regularly can stall decision-making on national issues.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these disruptions are particularly concerning. Many diaspora members closely follow parliamentary proceedings, especially those related to security, economic policy, and reconstruction efforts. A dysfunctional parliament can hinder progress on matters that directly affect family members back home and the broader Somali community.\n\nThe recurring lack of quorum also raises questions about the commitment of lawmakers and the effectiveness of the current parliamentary leadership. Without a functioning legislature, passing critical laws and providing oversight of the executive branch becomes difficult.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/07/kulankii-golaha-shacabka-oo-mar-kale-u-baaqday-koram-laaan/",
